<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides an overview of the
implementation details, followed by the results of our experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation Details</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we use our model to forecast a window based on the previous one. For additional information, we concatenate continuous inputs described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Dataset and Additional Model Inputs ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Diffusion-Based Generation and Imputation of Driving Scenarios from Limited Vehicle CAN Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a> channel-wise to the input. We use MSE as our loss function, an SGD optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0004 and a batch size of 32 for 100 epochs, resulting in a total training time of 3 hours on an NVIDIA A100 GPU. To evaluate the model, we generate 4096 windows and 16 laps per model with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=500</annotation></semantics></math> time steps. For the imputation with RePaint, we set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=r=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=r=5</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that there will always be some variation in the results due to the stochastic nature of the training and sampling process of diffusion models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our generation experiments using the three network architectures, LongConv, SSSD<sup id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">S4</sup>, and SSSD<sup id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">Mamba</sup>, including their network input configuration. All results are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-D Imputation ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Diffusion-Based Generation and Imputation of Driving Scenarios from Limited Vehicle CAN Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LongConv</span> In our first experiment, we use the LongConv architecture and input the zero-padded sensor data, the elevation, the track position embedding, and the vehicle parameters into the network. Since LongConv has no dedicated input for non-time series data, such as the vehicle parameters, we repeat them and stack them on top of the time series data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When generating only a single next window, LongConv is already able to outperform the training data in terms of the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MSE_{acc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MSE_{acc}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, there is a small but noticeable discontinuity between the windows and the difference in speed and swa to the original next window is relatively large. When generating a lap, this causes the model to diverge, after the generation a few windows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SSSD<sup id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">S4</sup></span> In our next experiment, we use SSSD<sup id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_sup">S4</sup> as our network architecture. We feed all additional inputs, e.g., the elevation, the vehicle data and the padding indicator into our improved version of SSSD’s condition input. We also use our proposed PCSP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The use of SSSD<sup id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_sup">S4</sup> reduces the <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MSE_{acc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MSE_{acc}</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.036 and the difference for the generated speed and swa in comparison to the original next window enabling the model to generate a full lap without diverging. The generated laps outperform the training data by far in terms of physical plausibility, evaluated by both the signs score and the <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MSE_{acc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MSE_{acc}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the speed and the swa differs significantly from the realistic range of values, exhibiting an unrealistic driving behavior where the car would not stay on track.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE III</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation of the imputation experiments. For all settings, we show the <math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MSE_{acc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MSE_{acc}</annotation></semantics></math> and the quantified continuity for each channel and the start (left) and end (right) of the imputed regions separately.</span></figcaption>
